<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarmatism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–18th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarmatism was the ideology and culture of the nobility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> from the sixteenth to the eighteenth centuries, based on the belief that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> descended from the ancient Sarmatians. It celebrated noble liberty, equality among nobles, Catholic piety and martial virtue, and shaped dress, portraiture and funeral customs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> critics blamed it for conservatism and xenophobia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarmatism.jpg" alt="Sarmatism" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanislaw_Antoni_Szczuka_(1652_1654-1710).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
